<BaseLayout title="Scheduler" description="SIGRUN scheduler: a 64-slot task table, timer-driven preemption every 10 ms, and a hand-written stack-based context switch.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Subsystem 3 · scheduler</p>
  <h1>Scheduler: preemption and the context switch</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Every 10&nbsp;ms the LAPIC timer fires, and inside the interrupt handler the kernel decides whether the
    next task should run. "Deciding" is trivial (round-robin); the real machinery is
    <code class="mono">switch_context</code> — 90 lines of assembly that swaps stacks mid-interrupt.
  </p>

  <h2>What preempts what</h2>
  <p>
    SIGRUN's scheduler is <strong>preemptive and timer-driven</strong>: no task has to cooperate.
    <code class="mono">tasks</code> live in a flat 64-slot <code class="mono">TaskTable</code> guarded by a
    <code class="mono">spin::Mutex</code>. Each has its own 8&nbsp;KiB kernel stack, a
    <code class="mono">Priority</code> (0 = highest … 255 = idle), and a state (ready / running / blocked /
    sleeping / terminated). The timer ISR calls <code class="mono">scheduler::tick()</code>; that picks the
    next ready, non-idle task and context-switches.
  </p>

