  <h2>The context switch, line by line</h2>
<pre><code><span class="c">// arch/x86_64/switch.rs — switch_context(old_rsp, new_rsp)</span>
global_asm!(
    "switch_context:",
    <span class="c">// Save what the callee is allowed to clobber... no — save what CALLER expects intact.</span>
    "pushfq",                  <span class="c">// RFLAGS (IF etc.)</span>
    "push r15", "push r14", "push r13", "push r12", "push rbp", "push rbx",
    <span class="c">// Park this task's stack pointer:</span>
    "mov qword ptr [rdi], rsp",   <span class="c">// *old_rsp = RSP   (rdi = &amp;Task::kernel_stack)</span>
    <span class="c">// Adopt the next task's stack:</span>
    "mov rsp, rsi",               <span class="c">// RSP = new_rsp   (rsi = next task's kernel_stack)</span>
    <span class="c">// Unwind that task's saved frame — its regs, its flags, its future:</span>
    "pop rbx", "pop rbp", "pop r12", "pop r13", "pop r14", "pop r15",
    "popfq",
    "ret",                        <span class="c">// pops the continuation address → returns INTO task B</span>
);</code></pre>
  <p>
    Why those registers? x86-64's calling convention says <code class="mono">rbx</code>,
    <code class="mono">rbp</code>, <code class="mono">r12</code>–<code class="mono">r15</code> are
    callee-saved — a function must preserve them. A context switch is the ultimate "call", so those six
    registers plus RFLAGS are the entire CPU state we must keep per task on the stack. Everything else
    (scratch registers, RIP via the return address) rides along for free in the frame.
  </p>

  <h2>Bootstrapping a fresh stack</h2>
  <p>
    A new task has never executed, so its stack must look like a task that was just switched out. The kernel
    fabricates that frame in <code class="mono">init_task_stack</code>: write the entry point where
    <code class="mono">ret</code> will pop it, zero the saved registers, set RFLAGS=<code class="mono">0x202</code>
    (interrupts on). The first context switch to a task "returns" into its entry function.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>tick(): lock, pick, drop the lock, switch</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c">// scheduler/mod.rs — called from the timer ISR (interrupts already off)</span>
pub fn tick() &#123;
    TICK_COUNT.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
    let mut table = TASK_TABLE.lock();
    let next_idx = match table.next_ready() &#123; Some(i) =&gt; i, None =&gt; return &#125;;
    if next_idx == current_idx &#123; return; &#125;        <span class="c">// nobody to switch to</span>
    let next_rsp = table.slots[next_idx].as_ref().unwrap().kernel_stack;
    let old_rsp_ptr: *mut u64 = if current_idx == usize::MAX &#123;
        &amp;raw mut BOOT_SAVED_RSP        <span class="c">// first switch out of boot — throw away</span>
    &#125; else &#123;
        &amp;mut table.slots[current_idx].as_mut().unwrap().kernel_stack
    &#125;;
    CURRENT_TASK_IDX.store(next_idx, Ordering::SeqCst);
    drop(table);                                  <span class="c">// MUST release the lock first</span>
    unsafe &#123; switch_context(old_rsp_ptr, next_rsp) &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
    <div class="callout warn">
      <span class="label">The deadlock you must never hit</span>
      <code class="mono">spin::Mutex</code> is <em>not reentrant</em>. Lock it twice in one call stack and the
      second <code class="mono">lock()</code> spins forever. Two concrete rules follow: (1) never lock
      TASK_TABLE twice in one path, (2) drop the lock <em>before</em> switching stacks — otherwise the mutex
      stays locked by a task that isn't running, and the next task that needs it deadlocks while
      <code class="mono">next_ready</code> can never return to release it.
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Round-robin, priorities, and the MLFQ shape</h2>
  <p>
    The live scheduler is a <strong>flat 64-slot round robin</strong>:
    <code class="mono">TaskTable::next_ready</code> scans circularly from the current slot, prefers non-idle
    tasks, and falls back to idle. <code class="mono">Priority</code> (0 = realtime range, 128 = default,
    255 = idle) mainly feeds the scan. There is also a <strong>32-level MLFQ runqueue</strong>
    (<code class="mono">scheduler/runqueue.rs</code>) with per-priority queues, but the code that actually runs
    is the task-table scan — the runqueue is present, tested, and ready to take over when priorities need
    teeth. The idle task (<code class="mono">Priority::IDLE</code>) just does
    <code class="mono">sti; hlt</code> in a loop.
  </p>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: scheduler</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Walk me through one preemption from the top.</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The LAPIC fires vector 32. The CPU pushes an interrupt frame (RIP/CS/RFLAGS/RSP/SS) and jumps to the
          IDT entry — the <code class="mono">timer</code> handler, declared <code class="mono">extern
          "x86-interrupt"</code> so the frame arrives as a typed argument. It sends EOI first (so the LAPIC can
          fire again even if we switch contexts), then <code class="mono">timer::on_tick()</code> advances the
          clock, then <code class="mono">scheduler::tick()</code> picks the next ready task, drops the table
          lock, and calls <code class="mono">switch_context</code>. The asm saves the running task's
          callee-saved registers and RSP into its <code class="mono">Task.kernel_stack</code>, loads the next
          task's RSP, pops its frame, and <code class="mono">ret</code> — which returns into B's code with
          interrupts still disabled until B's <code class="mono">popfq</code>/its own sti restores them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Why is EOI sent before the work?" — because the context switch may never return to this handler invocation. If EOI came after, the LAPIC could be stuck masking subsequent interrupts for a task that is no longer running.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>What exactly does <code class="mono">switch_context</code> save, and why those registers?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The six callee-saved registers (<code class="mono">rbx, rbp, r12–r15</code>) plus RFLAGS, and the RSP
          (stored via the out-pointer). RIP is not saved explicitly — it's the return address already on the
          stack, and <code class="mono">ret</code> pops the next task's continuation. Volatile registers
          (rax, rcx, rdx, rsi, rdi, r8–r11) don't survive a call anyway, so the ABI lets us ignore them. Real
          context switches additionally save FPU/SSE state (XSAVE) and, for separate address spaces, CR3.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Where does the very first switch go?" — <code class="mono">BOOT_SAVED_RSP</code> is a write-once throwaway slot used when <code class="mono">CURRENT_TASK_IDX</code> is <code class="mono">usize::MAX</code> (boot, and after <code class="mono">sys_exit</code> → <code class="mono">switch_to_idle</code>).</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>Why does the runloop scan a flat table instead of using the MLFQ queues?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Incremental development: the flat table is correct and simple for the current three kernel tasks +
          idle, and the MLFQ is a tested-but-orphaned upgrade path. The scan is O(64) worst case and keeps a
          single lock, which is the right trade at this scale. When priorities become load-bearing (e.g.
          driver threads vs interactive shell), the runqueue with its per-level arrays and boost logic is the
          intended replacement — a clean answer is "I built the data structure first and kept the simpler
          selector until the workloads needed it".
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>What happens if a task never blocks — can it starve everything?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          No, because preemption is timer-driven, not cooperative — <code class="mono">counter_task</code> is a
          pure tight loop and the timer still switches it out every 10&nbsp;ms. That's the point of the demo:
          CPU-bound work is preempted by the hardware timer, the task table scan never needs the task to yield.
          Voluntary yield exists too: <code class="mono">sys_sched_yield</code> simply calls the same
          <code class="mono">tick()</code> path.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

